#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# energy_drift

#% text
# `energy_drift(L, q, sol, [t, t0, t1])` gives the largest change `|E(t) - E(t0)|` of the energy function of `L` along the numerical solution `sol` (the rules of `n_dsolve_motion` or `n_dsolve`), sampled at 201 equally spaced times.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `points: k` samples `k + 1` times.

#% item
# For a Lagrangian that does not depend on `t` explicitly the energy is conserved exactly, so the drift measures the error of the integration.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Kepler's problem in polar coordinates:

#% cell
lk = (r'(t)^2 + r(t)^2*f'(t)^2)/2 + 1/r(t)

#% cell
s = n_dsolve_motion(lk, [r, f], [t, 0, 30], [1, 0], [0, 1.2])

#% cell
energy_drift(lk, [r, f], s, [t, 0, 30]) < 1.0e-6
#% output
#= true

